```java
import java.util.Arrays;

public class AnagramChecker {

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        String str1 = "listen";
        String str2 = "silent";

        boolean areAnagrams = checkIfAnagrams(str1, str2);
        if (areAnagrams) {
            System.out.println(str1 + " and " + str2 + " are anagrams.");
        } else {
            System.out.println(str1 + " and " + str2 + " are not anagrams.");
        }
    }

    public static boolean checkIfAnagrams(String str1, String str2) {
        str1 = str1.replaceAll("\\s", "");
        str2 = str2.replaceAll("\\s", "");

        if (str1.length() != str2.length()) {
            return false;
        } else {
            char[] arr1 = str1.toLowerCase().toCharArray();
            char[] arr2 = str2.toLowerCase().toCharArray();

            Arrays.sort(arr1);
            Arrays.sort(arr2);

            return Arrays.equals(arr1, arr2);
        }
    }
}
```

In this program, we first define two strings `str1` and `str2`. We then call the `checkIfAnagrams()` method and pass in these two strings.

The `checkIfAnagrams()` method first removes any white spaces from the input strings using the `replaceAll()` method. It then checks if the lengths of the strings are equal. If not, it returns `false` since they cannot be anagrams of each other. Otherwise, it converts both strings to lowercase and then converts them to character arrays using the `toCharArray()` method.

The `Arrays.sort()` method is used to sort both character arrays in ascending order. We then use the `Arrays.equals()` method to compare both sorted arrays. If they are equal, then the two strings are anagrams of each other, and the method returns `true`. Otherwise, it returns `false`.

Finally, we print a message indicating whether the two input strings are anagrams or not.

